# Linux Update Utility — Machine Readme

README.md is the short public guide. This machine-readme is the complete
technical, maintainer, and release-maintenance reference for Linux Update
Utility. It is intentionally more detailed than README.md and should stay in
sync whenever architecture, packaging, or supported behavior changes.

Last reviewed against the working tree: September 2026.

## Purpose

Linux Update Utility is a Linux-only Qt 6 desktop front end for the update commands already supplied by a host operating system. It does not replace a package manager. It detects installed package-manager executables, creates a readable shell script using their normal commands, and lets the GUI run that script.

| Package manager | Generated command | Privilege model |
| --- | --- | --- |
| pacman | pacman -Syu --noconfirm | One sudo group |
| apt | apt update && apt upgrade -y | One sudo group |
| dnf | dnf upgrade -y | One sudo group |
| snap | snap refresh | One sudo group |
| flatpak | flatpak update -y | Normal user |

Keep the project small, Linux-native, and transparent. Do not add a service, daemon, or abstraction layer merely to duplicate work that bash, sudo, and the host package manager already do.

### Functional boundaries

The project does not manage repositories, install development dependencies,
replace a distribution's update policy, or promise to make every package
manager safe in every configuration. It coordinates the host tools it finds.
That means package-manager output, prompts, failures, and repository policy
remain properties of the host system.

The updater has three distinct responsibilities that should remain separate:

| Layer | Responsibility | Must not do |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Qt GUI | User interaction, password prompt, process output, system information | Generate package-manager shell fragments. |
| C++ backend | Detect results, explicit command selection, update-script generation | Render GUI or install files system-wide. |
| bash / host tools | Executable detection, generated script execution, package updates | Carry application state or embed GUI logic. |

## Current status and release rule

The updater and separate setup utility have worked in Ubuntu and Fedora VM testing. The setup utility creates a normal application-menu entry, installs the updater to /usr/local/bin, and can update an existing install.

The current CMake development builds include:

* Seth Langer creator credit in the footer;
* a small white circular information button with a three-line notice;
* the complete GPLv3 in the root LICENSE file;
* a Settings menu that can uninstall the system-wide application while preserving user-local data;
* persistent Light, Dark, Green Phosphor, Amber CRT, and Classic Blue choices in the Settings > Theme menu;
* Update Existing Install in the setup utility;
* safer AppImage replacement: write a complete .new file, then rename it over the old AppImage.

### Critical: the embedded payload is separate

package-setup-utility.sh copies release-input/Linux_Update_Utility-x86_64.AppImage into the setup AppImage unchanged. It never rebuilds or inspects that file.

The current local payload was replaced on September 2, 2026 with a newer
known-good updater AppImage (SHA-256
`37ec707f5ea6485f92161e16a02141ded0a670d8aaf3d89b14ee5667e5c72964`). It
contains the current footer and compact legal-notice UI. A setup AppImage was
