## Architecture

    MainWindow Qt GUI
            |
            +-- runs linux-update-utility (C++ backend)
            |      +-- runs scripts/detect-package-managers.sh
            |            +-- command -v pacman / flatpak / apt / dnf / snap
            |
            +-- backend creates a readable update shell script in the user's
            |   Qt application-data directory
            |
            +-- GUI runs that script with bash, supplies one password over
                stdin only if sudo -S is present, and streams output to the UI

### Updater runtime flow

1. The user selects Generate Script or Update System.
2. MainWindow starts the C++ backend beside the GUI executable with the application root as its working directory.
3. The GUI passes a writable output path through LINUX_UPDATE_SCRIPT_OUTPUT. It comes from QStandardPaths::AppLocalDataLocation.
4. The backend invokes bash ./scripts/detect-package-managers.sh and writes the script for the package managers it finds.
5. Generate Script reports success. Update System regenerates the script, prompts for a password only if sudo -S is present, then runs bash followed by the generated-script path.
6. The GUI streams merged standard output/error into a QPlainTextEdit. It clears the in-memory password after writing it to the child process.

The script is human-readable by design. Never write it inside a read-only AppImage filesystem.

### Setup utility runtime flow

1. setup-main.cpp creates SetupWindow.
2. A packaged setup AppImage uses its embedded payload at usr/share/linux-update-utility/payload/Linux_Update_Utility-x86_64.AppImage. A development setup executable may use an AppImage beside itself.
3. SetupWindow stages the payload in a user-writable temporary file, so root does not need to access a FUSE-mounted AppImage.
4. It creates temporary icon/desktop files, requests the password once, and runs a small sudo -S bash installation command.
5. It installs these files:

        /usr/local/bin/linux-update-utility.AppImage
        /usr/share/applications/linux-update-utility.desktop
        /usr/share/icons/hicolor/scalable/apps/linux-update-utility.svg

6. When the updater already exists, Update Existing Install writes a complete .new AppImage then renames it into place. It refreshes the launcher/icon without intentionally deleting user-local application data.

### Uninstall flow

1. The user selects Uninstall Linux Update Utility from the Settings menu.
2. The main window confirms the scope and requests administrator authorization.
3. The application removes only the installed AppImage, desktop entry, and icon created by the setup utility.
4. User-local application data, including generated scripts, remains in place.
5. After a successful uninstall, the running application displays confirmation and closes.

### Appearance settings

The Settings > Theme menu provides mutually exclusive Light, Dark, Green
Phosphor, Amber CRT, and Classic Blue choices. The three latter choices use
high-contrast palettes inspired by classic computer terminals.
The selected theme applies to the full Qt application, including menus and
dialogs, and is saved with QSettings for the next launch. Light is the default
when no preference has been saved.

Do not use /bin as the install destination. A minimal Ubuntu VM showed that /bin can be absent or system-managed; creating it incorrectly can break the expected /bin/sh path.

### CMake targets and packaged layout

CMake builds three independent executables into the selected build directory:

| Target | Role |
| --- | --- |
| linux-update-utility | Command-generation backend. |
| linux-updater-app | Main end-user updater GUI. |
| luu-setup-utility | Separate installer/updater GUI. |

The updater AppImage layout installed by CMake contains the main GUI and
backend under usr/bin, the detection helper under scripts, the desktop file
under usr/share/applications, and the SVG icon under the hicolor icon path.
MainWindow depends on that relationship: it starts the backend beside itself
and gives it the application root as the working directory so the relative
scripts/detect-package-managers.sh path remains valid.

This relative-path relationship is intentional but packaging-sensitive. Any
future change to the AppDir layout must be tested by generating a script from
the packaged updater, not just from a development build.

